<DocsLayout title="CLI Reference" description="RelayCore command line interface">
  <h1>CLI Reference</h1>

  <p>The <code>relay-core</code> binary provides a full-featured command-line interface for running the proxy, inspecting traffic, and managing rules, scripts, and certificates. For the full schema run <code>relay-core --help</code> or <code>relay-core &lt;command&gt; --help</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="lifecycle">Daemon lifecycle</h2>
  <p>One long-lived daemon owns the engine and the proxy lifecycle: the single state lives in it, it publishes <code>$RELAY_DATA_DIR/daemon.json</code> for clients to discover, and it serves the control API, the MCP endpoint, and the embedded Web UI. <code>relay-core status</code> prints the page URL. Turn the page off with <code>--no-web</code> or <code>[daemon] webui = false</code>. Every other surface — CLI, MCP bridge, Web UI, TUI — is a client of it. The proxy still starts only when a command or tool asks for it.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core start      Start the daemon and the proxy (idempotent; attaches if already up)
relay-core stop       Stop the proxy only; the daemon, history and rules stay
relay-core restart    Restart the proxy
relay-core status     Daemon / proxy phase and port / MCP and Web UI URLs / last change
relay-core shutdown   Stop the proxy and the daemon (returns once the registry is clean)
relay-core config     Read, show and seed the configuration file</code></pre>

  <p><code>start</code> is idempotent: an already-running daemon is attached to, an already-running proxy reports the port it listens on, and a taken port fails with <code>start_failed</code> rather than reporting success for a proxy that never bound. <code>status</code> also reports the last lifecycle change and who asked for it, e.g. <code>stopped by cli:relay-core stop</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="run">run</h2>
  <p>Run that same daemon in the <strong>foreground</strong> — not a second engine, just one that does not detach and ends when you quit. It publishes the same manifest and serves the same control API and MCP endpoint; with <code>--ui</code> the TUI is its client, reading the same event stream every other client reads.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core run [options]

Listen &amp; lifecycle:
  -l, --listen &lt;ADDR&gt;           Proxy listen address (default 127.0.0.1:8080)
      --udp-tproxy-port &lt;PORT&gt;   Enable UDP TPROXY on this port (Linux only)
      --transparent               Enable transparent proxy mode (macOS PF / Linux TPROXY)

TLS interception:
      --ca-cert &lt;PATH&gt;           CA certificate path
      --ca-key &lt;PATH&gt;            CA private key path (pair with --ca-cert)

Rules &amp; scripts:
      --rules &lt;PATH&gt;             Load rules from file (JSON / YAML)
      --script &lt;PATH&gt;            Load Deno script file
      --script-watch              Watch script file for hot reload
      --script-env-allow &lt;LIST&gt;  Comma-separated env var names scripts may read
      --script-fetch-allow &lt;LIST&gt; Hosts relay.fetch may call (comma-separated; * is any host).
                                  Setting this is what enables relay.fetch, and it must be set
                                  before the script is loaded

TUI:
      --ui                        Enable interactive TUI mode
      --web                       Serve the Web UI even when the config file turned it off
      --no-web                    Do not serve the embedded Web UI
      --theme &lt;NAME&gt;             TUI color preset: relay, slate, high-contrast
                                  (overrides RELAY_CORE_TUI_THEME and config)

Flow recording:
      --save-stream &lt;PATH&gt;       Append every flow update as JSONL to this file (bodies and
                                  WebSocket messages included)

Control API and MCP:
      --api-port &lt;PORT&gt;          Control API / Web UI port (default 8082; an ephemeral port
                                  is used when it is taken)
      --api-bind &lt;ADDR&gt;          Bind address (default 127.0.0.1)
      --api-token &lt;TOKEN&gt;        Bearer token (the daemon generates one and publishes it in the
                                  manifest)
      --api-cors &lt;ORIGINS&gt;       CORS allowed origins (comma-separated)
      --mcp-port &lt;PORT&gt;          Also serve the MCP endpoint from this process (default 18083)

Upstream proxy:
      --upstream &lt;URL&gt;           Parent proxy URL (http:// or https://)
      --upstream-auth-user &lt;U&gt;   Username for upstream Basic auth
                                  (password: RELAYCORE_UPSTREAM_PASSWORD env)
      --upstream-bypass &lt;LIST&gt;   Hosts to bypass upstream
      --upstream-fail-open        Fall back to direct if upstream unreachable</code></pre>

  <p>See <a href="/en/docs/tui">TUI</a> for the interactive terminal UI, <a href="/en/docs/proxy-modes">Proxy Modes</a> for deployment options, and <a href="/en/docs/configuration">Configuration</a> for environment variables and data directory layout.</p>

  <h2 id="ca">ca</h2>
  <p>Manage the CA certificate for HTTPS interception. See <a href="/en/docs/certificates">Certificates</a> for platform-specific install instructions, Windows .cer export, and CI/container setup.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core ca &lt;subcommand&gt;

Subcommands:
  generate [--force]            Generate CA cert and key (default ~/.relay-core/)
  install                       Install CA to system trust store (macOS)
  uninstall                     Remove CA from system trust store (macOS)
  status                        Show CA paths, existence, and trust status
  export [-o PATH] [--der]      Export CA certificate
                                --der produces a binary .cer for Windows import</code></pre>

  <h2 id="rules">rules</h2>
  <p>Manage interception rules.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core rules &lt;subcommand&gt;

Subcommands:
  validate &lt;file&gt;              Validate a rules file (JSON / YAML)
  print &lt;file&gt; [--format F]     Print rules in normalized form (json | yaml, default yaml)
  test &lt;file&gt; --flow &lt;flow.json&gt;
                                Run a sample flow through the rule set
  list [--api-url &lt;URL&gt;]       List rules from a running proxy via the HTTP API
                                (default http://127.0.0.1:18082)</code></pre>

  <h2 id="scripts">scripts</h2>
  <p>Manage Deno-based request/response modification scripts.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core scripts &lt;subcommand&gt;

Subcommands:
  validate &lt;file&gt;              Load a script into the Deno engine to check syntax
  run-once &lt;file&gt; --flow &lt;f&gt;   Execute the script once against a sample flow JSON
  init [dir]                    Scaffold an esbuild + tsconfig project (default .)
  build [entry] [-o OUT]        Bundle the entry script (default src/index.ts -&gt; dist/bundle.js)
  dev [entry] [-o OUT]          esbuild --watch mode for local development</code></pre>

  <p>The <code>init</code> subcommand creates a starter <code>src/index.ts</code> and <code>tsconfig.json</code>. <code>build</code> / <code>dev</code> use <a href="https://esbuild.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">esbuild</a> to produce a single bundle that <code>--script</code> can load.</p>

  <h2 id="flows">flows</h2>
  <p>Inspect captured traffic: a listing of recent flows by default, a live stream with <code>--follow</code>. Both read the running daemon's control API, discovered from its manifest — there is no URL to configure by hand.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core flows [options]

Connection:
      --api-url &lt;URL&gt;           Control API base URL (discovered from the daemon manifest when
                                omitted)
      --follow                   Stream new flows; filters do not apply to a live stream, so the
                                 two cannot be combined

Listing filters:
      --host &lt;HOST&gt;             Substring match on host
      --path &lt;PATH&gt;             Substring match on path
      --method &lt;METHOD&gt;         HTTP method (GET, POST, ...)
      --status-min &lt;N&gt;          Minimum status code
      --status-max &lt;N&gt;          Maximum status code
      --has-error                Only flows with errors
      --websocket                Only WebSocket flows
      --filter &lt;EXPR&gt;           Filter expression (see syntax below)
      --limit &lt;N&gt;               Max results (1-200, default 50)

Output:
      --output &lt;FORMAT&gt;         table, json, jsonl (default table)</code></pre>

  <h2 id="metrics">metrics</h2>
  <p>Display runtime metrics. The proxy is queried over HTTP at <code>--proxy-url</code> (default <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080</code>).</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core metrics [options]

      --proxy-url &lt;URL&gt;         Proxy metrics endpoint (default http://127.0.0.1:8080)
      --output &lt;FORMAT&gt;         table, json (default table)

Reported metrics include flows total / in memory / dropped, intercepts pending,
WebSocket pending messages, oldest intercept / WS message age, rule execution
errors, audit event totals and failures, and the lagged counters for both
flow and audit event channels.</code></pre>

  <p>For a Prometheus-formatted scrape, use the HTTP API endpoint <code>GET /api/v1/metrics/prometheus</code> (see <a href="/en/docs/api#metrics">HTTP API</a>).</p>

  <h2 id="analyze">analyze</h2>
  <p>Analyze a traffic dump (from <code>--save-stream</code>) or a HAR export offline. A recorded file may hold a whole flow per line or a flow update per line — the new recording is lossless and includes incremental updates — and both are read.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core analyze --file &lt;PATH&gt; [options]

      --file &lt;PATH&gt;             Path to JSONL flow dump or HAR file
      --format &lt;FORMAT&gt;         jsonl (default) or har
      --output &lt;FORMAT&gt;         table (default) or json
      --top-n &lt;N&gt;               Number of slow requests to show (default 10)

Prints a host histogram, method histogram, status histogram, top-N slowest
requests, and clustered error groups.</code></pre>

  <h2 id="proxy">proxy</h2>
  <p>Manage transparent proxy on macOS (PF) or Linux (TPROXY). Compiled only with the <code>transparent-macos</code> or <code>transparent-linux</code> features.</p>
  <pre><code>relay-core proxy &lt;subcommand&gt;

Subcommands:
  generate [--port P] [--interface I] [-o OUT]
                                Write PF / TPROXY config to OUT (default stdout)
  load --port P [--interface I] Activate the rules (requires sudo)
  unload                        Deactivate the rules (requires sudo)
  status                        Show current rules</code></pre>

  <h2 id="env">Environment variables</h2>
  <pre><code>RUST_LOG                        Log filter (e.g. info, debug, trace). RELAY_LOG in the CLI help is not read
RELAY_DATA_DIR                  Data directory (default ~/.relay-core)
RELAY_CA_CERT                   CA cert path (pair with RELAY_CA_KEY)
RELAY_CA_KEY                    CA key path (pair with RELAY_CA_CERT)
RELAY_CORE_TUI_THEME            TUI preset when --theme is omitted
                                (relay, slate, high-contrast)
RELAYCORE_UPSTREAM_PASSWORD     Upstream proxy Basic auth password
                                (used with --upstream-auth-user)
RELAY_API_PORT                  Control API / Web UI port override
RELAY_MCP_PORT                  MCP endpoint port override for the daemon
RELAY_PROXY_PORT                Proxy port override
RELAY_PROXY_IDLE_TIMEOUT        Seconds without traffic before the proxy stops (0 = never)
RELAY_MCP_URL / RELAY_MCP_TOKEN Endpoint and token the MCP bridge should use directly
RELAY_MCP_NO_AUTOSTART          Set to 1 to forbid the bridge from starting a daemon</code></pre>

  <p>See <a href="/en/docs/configuration">Configuration</a> for the full data directory layout and path precedence.</p>
</DocsLayout>
